line-depth
==========

Print the *commit depth* of lines of a file: how many distinct commits in a
revision range touched a line, taking the largest value over the lines
selected.

    line-depth <rev-range> <start>[,<end>]:<file>
    line-depth <rev-range> max:<file>

For example, `line-depth origin/main..HEAD 10,40:foo.c` prints the highest
depth among lines 10 to 40 of `foo.c` as of `HEAD`, and `max:foo.c` covers
every line of the file.  The only output on success is one integer.

How it works
------------

Nothing here tracks history itself.  The depth of a line is the number of
distinct commits that `git log -L<n>,<n>:<file> <rev-range>` reports, and the
result for a range or file is the maximum of those per-line depths -- not the
number of commits touching the range as a whole.  Two lines edited by two
different commits therefore give 1, not 2.  The commit that adds a line
counts as a touch.

What it does not cover
----------------------

- Line numbers refer to the file at the tip of `<rev-range>`.  Lines deleted
  before the tip, and lines added and removed inside the range, are not
  measured.  A depth of 0 for an emptied file does not say anything about the
  commits that deleted its lines.
- It follows Git's `-L` history, including its merge handling: when a merge
  matches one parent for the line, only that parent is followed, so side
  commits can go uncounted.  Counts can also be too high: when a hunk is
  replaced, split or joined, every resulting line inherits the history of the
  whole old hunk.
- Renames are followed according to `diff.renames`, so the same history can
  give different numbers under different configuration.
- Binary files are rejected.  Symbolic links and submodules, and shallow
  repositories (history cut off at the shallow boundary undercounts), are not
  handled specially yet.
- Only plain numbers are accepted for `<start>` and `<end>`, with
  `<start>` <= `<end>`, and an end past the end of the file is an error.

An empty revision set (for example `X..X`) prints 0 once the revisions, path
and lines have been validated.  Any failure from Git, such as an unknown
revision or a missing path, makes the command fail with a message and no
output on stdout.

For reviewing a branch, use the tip of the base branch as the left side
(`origin/main..HEAD`).  Using the merge base instead also counts commits from
the base that were merged into the branch.

Tests
-----

    make -C t

The tests in `t/t9420-line-depth-characterize.sh` run `git log -L` alone and
record the behavior this tool relies on.
